1
00:00:00,750 --> 00:00:04,800
Most cancer drugs fail before they reach a patient.

2
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:07,720
Part of the problem is the testing.

3
00:00:07,970 --> 00:00:11,522
For decades, cancer cells have been grown flat, in a

4
00:00:11,322 --> 00:00:12,360
plastic dish.

5
00:00:12,660 --> 00:00:14,680
Real tumours aren't flat.

6
00:00:15,380 --> 00:00:19,505
So labs now grow something closer to the real thing:

7
00:00:19,305 --> 00:00:22,825
tiny 3D copies of a patient's tumour, called

8
00:00:22,625 --> 00:00:23,580
organoids.

9
00:00:24,780 --> 00:00:28,417
This year, two leading cancer centres used organoids

10
00:00:28,217 --> 00:00:31,919
to switch off every gene, one at a time, and find the

11
00:00:31,719 --> 00:00:34,100
ones a cancer can't live without.

12
00:00:34,700 --> 00:00:38,165
Hundreds of genes mattered more in organoids than in

13
00:00:37,965 --> 00:00:41,304
flat cells, and those are now being put forward as

14
00:00:41,104 --> 00:00:42,120
drug targets.

15
00:00:43,420 --> 00:00:44,710
Here's the catch.

16
00:00:45,010 --> 00:00:47,740
Organoids change two things at once.

17
00:00:48,340 --> 00:00:52,040
One is the shape. 3D instead of flat.

18
00:00:52,640 --> 00:00:54,950
The other is the food.

19
00:00:55,250 --> 00:00:58,910
Cells in a dish live in a liquid called the medium.

20
00:00:59,210 --> 00:01:02,922
Flat cells usually get serum, the nutrient-rich part

21
00:01:02,722 --> 00:01:03,530
of blood.

22
00:01:03,830 --> 00:01:07,667
Organoids get a clean, lab-made medium with almost no

23
00:01:07,467 --> 00:01:08,490
cholesterol.

24
00:01:09,190 --> 00:01:12,968
So when a gene matters more in an organoid, which

25
00:01:12,768 --> 00:01:14,210
change caused it?

26
00:01:14,560 --> 00:01:16,920
The shape, which is the whole point?

27
00:01:17,170 --> 00:01:19,100
Or just the diet?

28
00:01:20,400 --> 00:01:23,460
The data to tell them apart already existed.

29
00:01:23,760 --> 00:01:27,134
A big public database had flat cells grown on the

30
00:01:26,934 --> 00:01:28,170
organoid medium.

31
00:01:28,470 --> 00:01:31,140
Same diet, no 3D shape.

32
00:01:31,840 --> 00:01:35,477
If a gene matters more there too, the shape can't

33
00:01:35,277 --> 00:01:37,230
explain it. The food can.

34
00:01:39,430 --> 00:01:44,500
We ran that check on all 17,525 genes.

35
00:01:45,100 --> 00:01:49,080
456 clearly behave differently in organoids.

36
00:01:49,480 --> 00:01:53,775
So how many of those 456 also change when the only

37
00:01:53,575 --> 00:01:56,150
thing you change is the food?

38
00:01:56,750 --> 00:02:00,055
If organoids were finding something new, that number

39
00:01:59,855 --> 00:02:01,010
should be small.

40
00:02:03,110 --> 00:02:04,910
387.

41
00:02:05,410 --> 00:02:07,150
Eighty-five percent.

42
00:02:09,150 --> 00:02:10,890
Here's why that matters.

43
00:02:11,290 --> 00:02:14,793
These are exactly the kind of genes being put forward

44
00:02:14,593 --> 00:02:16,040
as new drug targets.

45
00:02:16,440 --> 00:02:19,594
But for most of them, ordinary flat cells show the

46
00:02:19,394 --> 00:02:22,370
same change, as soon as they get the same food.

47
00:02:22,970 --> 00:02:26,942
So the effect may not come from the tumour's 3D shape

48
00:02:26,742 --> 00:02:29,860
at all. It may come from what we feed it.

49
00:02:30,760 --> 00:02:34,333
And in a patient, a tumour is fed by blood, which is

50
00:02:34,133 --> 00:02:35,630
full of cholesterol.

51
00:02:35,980 --> 00:02:39,626
So a weakness that only shows up on a diet with almost

52
00:02:39,426 --> 00:02:42,880
no cholesterol may not exist in the patient at all.

53
00:02:44,280 --> 00:02:47,760
On this chart, each dot is one of those genes.

54
00:02:48,010 --> 00:02:51,215
If a dot sits on the dashed line, flat cells on

55
00:02:51,015 --> 00:02:54,604
organoid food changed it just as much as the organoid

56
00:02:54,404 --> 00:02:54,860
did.

57
00:02:55,210 --> 00:02:57,480
Most of them sit right on that line.

58
00:02:58,280 --> 00:03:00,530
The clearest case is cholesterol.

59
00:03:00,830 --> 00:03:05,073
Organoid food has almost none, so the cells have to

60
00:03:04,873 --> 00:03:09,116
make their own. That makes the genes that build it,

61
00:03:08,916 --> 00:03:11,890
like SQLE, suddenly look essential.

62
00:03:12,790 --> 00:03:16,381
That leaves 69 genes that don't clearly follow the

63
00:03:16,181 --> 00:03:16,720
food.

64
00:03:17,020 --> 00:03:20,530
For 68 of them, the data can't say either way.

65
00:03:20,830 --> 00:03:24,270
Just one leans toward the shape, and not strongly.

66
00:03:24,870 --> 00:03:30,930
Three of them, SCAP, DDX3X and ITGB1, were among

67
00:03:30,730 --> 00:03:33,250
Sanger's top picks.

68
00:03:33,500 --> 00:03:36,758
They're the more credible leads: the ones still worth

69
00:03:36,558 --> 00:03:37,220
chasing.

70
00:03:38,520 --> 00:03:42,300
This is a pattern in existing data, not proof.

71
00:03:42,700 --> 00:03:44,760
But there's a simple way to check.

72
00:03:45,060 --> 00:03:48,050
Put the cholesterol back into the organoid's food.

73
00:03:48,450 --> 00:03:51,939
If these genes stop mattering, it was the food. If

74
00:03:51,739 --> 00:03:55,030
they still matter, they're stronger candidates.

75
00:03:55,530 --> 00:03:58,920
It takes about two weeks, and no new screen.

76
00:04:00,020 --> 00:04:02,920
We also tried hard to prove ourselves wrong.

77
00:04:03,270 --> 00:04:06,510
We checked the result on patient samples the analysis

78
00:04:06,310 --> 00:04:07,370
had never seen.

79
00:04:07,620 --> 00:04:12,000
We reran it 150 times on reshuffled data.

80
00:04:12,250 --> 00:04:15,698
Five rounds of independent review checked the code and

81
00:04:15,498 --> 00:04:16,360
the claims.

82
00:04:16,710 --> 00:04:20,383
And when one early finding didn't hold up, we dropped

83
00:04:20,183 --> 00:04:20,580
it.

84
00:04:21,880 --> 00:04:25,440
All of this is free at itsthemedium.com.

85
00:04:25,940 --> 00:04:29,489
Look up any gene and get a simple answer: does it

86
00:04:29,289 --> 00:04:31,130
follow the food, or not?

87
00:04:31,530 --> 00:04:34,969
Researchers can paste their own list of genes and see

88
00:04:34,769 --> 00:04:37,720
in seconds which ones look like diet effects.

89
00:04:38,420 --> 00:04:42,280
Muse, Meta's AI model, is the research assistant.

90
00:04:42,580 --> 00:04:46,256
Ask it a question and it searches the full table with

91
00:04:46,056 --> 00:04:49,733
its own tools, shows every step, and links every gene

92
00:04:49,533 --> 00:04:50,520
to the data.

93
00:04:51,020 --> 00:04:54,233
And Muse ran the research loop behind this whole

94
00:04:54,033 --> 00:04:54,610
audit.

95
00:04:55,910 --> 00:04:58,720
Organoids are still a big step forward.

96
00:04:58,970 --> 00:05:02,240
We just need to check what we're feeding them.

97
00:05:03,040 --> 00:05:04,290
It's the Medium.

98
00:05:04,790 --> 00:05:06,710
itsthemedium.com

